gosh seam 10: An IPv6 socket does without the IPv4 path-MTU option.

For:   REQ17/PATH-8. A server with an IPv6 address alone is reached as one with IPv4 is; on
       Windows (Cygwin and MSYS2) the IPv4 option IP_MTU_DISCOVER is refused on an IPv6 socket
       with EINVAL, so a session to an IPv6 address could not be opened at all. Since v10 gosh
       did not carry this; v16 carries it again (client, 2026-11-15: "carry the fix").
What:  the option's failure is fatal only on an IPv4 socket.
Where: Connection::Socket::Socket (network.cc).
Kind:  one condition gains a term, and a comment. Nothing is moved.
From:  the fork at mosh-1.4.0-pf.7, commit b60a373 ("Let an IPv6 socket do without the IPv4
       path-MTU option"), the same lines. Upstream's pull request 1442 makes the same change; when
       the pin moves past it, this patch goes.
Pin:   upstream mosh at decd9b705eb81626f694335b8d5940538beb06da; cut against the result of the patch before it.

--- a/src/network/network.cc
+++ b/src/network/network.cc
@@ -157,7 +157,13 @@
   /* Disable path MTU discovery */
 #ifdef HAVE_IP_MTU_DISCOVER
   int flag = IP_PMTUDISC_DONT;
-  if ( setsockopt( _fd, IPPROTO_IP, IP_MTU_DISCOVER, &flag, sizeof flag ) < 0 ) {
+  if ( setsockopt( _fd, IPPROTO_IP, IP_MTU_DISCOVER, &flag, sizeof flag ) < 0 && family == AF_INET ) {
+    /*
+     * This is an IPv4 option.  Linux takes it on an IPv6 socket as well,
+     * but not every system does (Cygwin and MSYS2 say EINVAL), and an
+     * IPv6 socket can do without it: we never send a datagram larger
+     * than every IPv6 path must carry.
+     */
     throw NetworkException( "setsockopt", errno );
   }
 #endif
